  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday Night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1032 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nForecast surface analyses depict a large, occluded upper low\ntraversing the eastern CONUS on Saturday morning. We'll start\nseeing impacts from this in the form of showers and the occasional\nthunderstorm early Saturday morning as this system approaches.\nMoving toward the afternoon hours, the actual cold front will \neventually catch up during peak heating, providing enough forcing\nto spark isolated stronger thunderstorms along the front. An \nearly look at parameter space indicates a weakly sheared \nenvironment with sfc-500 mb shear on the order of 20 kts. This is \nplenty to hold a few clusters of thunderstorms together \n(especially with ~1500 J/kg of CAPE) along the cold front, but not\nenough for supercellular behavior. In terms of a severe weather \nthreat, expect mainly disorganized clusters capable of producing \ngusty winds on their outflows in the afternoon and evening.\n\nThe more evident threat for Saturday is heavy rainfall. Due to the\nlocation of the upper trough being displaced just to our west,\nwe'll have plenty of upper level divergence throughout the day,\ngiving way to deep, moist convection. PWATs are also forecast to\nbe around 2 to 2.3 inches all day Saturday, which is nearing the \nupper echelon of climatology for early August. This will make for\nvery efficient rainfall producing storms before and during the\nFROPA. Though the front itself will have slow eastward progress,\nthe storm motions themselves will likely be following the boundary\nfrom southwest to northeast, creating a potential training \nscenario all day. Combined with training storms, these heavy \nrainfall rates will pose an elevated flash flooding risk for \nMiddle TN on Saturday.\n\nAfter the front passes early Sunday, humidity and temperatures\nbriefly drop to pleasant levels for early August. Unfortunately\nfor cooler weather lovers, not for long. By next week, the upper\nlevel pattern becomes more zonal and seasonably hot \ntemperatures/humidity return for the rest of the week.",
